WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.265
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:00:05.266 --> 00:00:06.945
Welcome to the afternoon session.

00:00:06.946 --> 00:00:09.285
We have two speakers now,

00:00:09.286 --> 00:00:10.800
both talks for 20 minutes.

00:00:10.801 --> 00:00:13.050
The first one is Suchin
Gururangan, is that,

00:00:13.051 --> 00:00:13.680
&gt;&gt; Yup.

00:00:13.681 --> 00:00:16.500
&gt;&gt; Close? With
Annotation Artifacts

00:00:16.501 --> 00:00:18.555
in Natural Language
and Inference Data.

00:00:18.556 --> 00:00:21.255
&gt;&gt; All right. Hey, everyone.
My name is Suchin,

00:00:21.256 --> 00:00:22.935
a graduate student in
University of Washington.

00:00:22.936 --> 00:00:24.810
This is going to be project
called Annotation Artifacts

00:00:24.811 --> 00:00:26.280
in Natural Language
Inference Data.

00:00:26.281 --> 00:00:28.635
Here with my co-first
author Swabha Swayamdipta,

00:00:28.636 --> 00:00:30.885
and this is joint
work with Omer Levy,

00:00:30.886 --> 00:00:33.480
Roy Schwartz, Sam Bowman
and Noah Smith.

00:00:33.481 --> 00:00:38.595
So, who here works with
crowdsource annotations,

00:00:38.596 --> 00:00:42.150
or maybe works at datasets
on crowdsource annotations?

00:00:42.151 --> 00:00:44.235
Yeah. So, this is
a really popular,

00:00:44.236 --> 00:00:46.050
sort of area of NLP,

00:00:46.051 --> 00:00:49.245
and it really drives
a lot of modern NLP.

00:00:49.246 --> 00:00:52.200
So, we're usually
given some text,

00:00:52.201 --> 00:00:54.600
we have a model, and we
have some output here.

00:00:54.601 --> 00:00:57.555
It's easy to forget

00:00:57.556 --> 00:00:59.265
that there are humans behind

00:00:59.266 --> 00:01:01.695
the text and the data that
we feed our models to.

00:01:01.696 --> 00:01:03.960
Those annotators,
because they are human,

00:01:03.961 --> 00:01:05.725
they have their own biases.

00:01:05.726 --> 00:01:07.710
They have their own beliefs
about the world.

00:01:07.711 --> 00:01:09.120
They have personal motives.

00:01:09.121 --> 00:01:10.980
They have their own
cultural backgrounds,

00:01:10.981 --> 00:01:14.235
and this affects
the distributions

00:01:14.236 --> 00:01:16.450
that we see in our data.

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:19.380
There's been a lot of
conversation about how biases

00:01:19.381 --> 00:01:21.720
affects downstream,
some subtle applications,

00:01:21.721 --> 00:01:23.250
but sort of long-running theme of

00:01:23.251 --> 00:01:25.380
this talk is that biases

00:01:25.381 --> 00:01:26.760
also affect how we evaluate

00:01:26.761 --> 00:01:29.610
models or design of our tasks.

00:01:29.611 --> 00:01:31.950
So, the story sort

00:01:31.951 --> 00:01:33.720
of starts with the natural
language inference task,

00:01:33.721 --> 00:01:35.925
which I think a lot of people
here are familiar with.

00:01:35.926 --> 00:01:37.620
Basically, you're
given two sentences,

00:01:37.621 --> 00:01:38.954
a premise and a hypothesis,

00:01:38.955 --> 00:01:40.530
and the goal is to
determine whether

00:01:40.531 --> 00:01:42.780
that premise entails
that hypothesis.

00:01:42.781 --> 00:01:44.235
So, there are three categories.

00:01:44.236 --> 00:01:46.950
So, for example, you're
given, on the premise,

00:01:46.951 --> 00:01:48.800
North West NLP workshop

00:01:48.801 --> 00:01:51.150
which is being held at
Microsoft headquarters.

00:01:51.151 --> 00:01:52.650
You have an entailment category,

00:01:52.651 --> 00:01:54.060
the workshop is in
Redmond because

00:01:54.061 --> 00:01:55.740
that's definitely true
given this premise.

00:01:55.741 --> 00:01:57.360
A contradiction that
Amazon assessing

00:01:57.361 --> 00:01:59.069
the workshop because
that can't be true,

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:01.290
and neutral example that

00:02:01.291 --> 00:02:03.885
Satya Nadella is attending
the workshop at Memphis here.

00:02:03.886 --> 00:02:05.955
But, so these are

00:02:05.956 --> 00:02:08.190
relatively well-defined
classes and

00:02:08.191 --> 00:02:09.975
it's really up for annotation.

00:02:09.976 --> 00:02:12.360
We've had a lot of
really great performance

00:02:12.361 --> 00:02:14.430
on this task over
the past few years.

00:02:14.431 --> 00:02:16.380
The Stanford NLI and
multiNLI datasets

00:02:16.381 --> 00:02:18.060
released in past couple years,

00:02:18.061 --> 00:02:19.725
and people have been getting

00:02:19.726 --> 00:02:23.770
scores in the 70s and 80s
across these two datasets.

00:02:23.771 --> 00:02:26.260
So, a newcomer to
this particular area

00:02:26.261 --> 00:02:28.100
of NLP might see
these scores and be like,

00:02:28.101 --> 00:02:29.590
"Okay, there's not
really much else to

00:02:29.591 --> 00:02:31.480
do in NLI, and what
am I doing here?"

00:02:31.481 --> 00:02:32.860
But I think the results of

00:02:32.861 --> 00:02:35.805
this paper kind of show
that there is a lot to do.

00:02:35.806 --> 00:02:39.380
Most NLI models, especially
the top-performing ones,

00:02:39.381 --> 00:02:41.120
sort of represent
both premise and

00:02:41.121 --> 00:02:43.610
the hypothesis in some joint way,

00:02:43.611 --> 00:02:45.400
feat that joint
representations down

00:02:45.401 --> 00:02:47.470
to a classifier to predict
those three labels.

00:02:47.471 --> 00:02:50.605
What we found very
interestingly is that if you

00:02:50.606 --> 00:02:52.855
completely disregard the premise

00:02:52.856 --> 00:02:54.280
and only consider the hypothesis,

00:02:54.281 --> 00:02:57.070
only feed the hypothesis
into that classifier,

00:02:57.071 --> 00:02:58.460
and make the same
predictions, you can do

00:02:58.461 --> 00:03:00.895
really well on this task.

00:03:00.896 --> 00:03:03.320
The classifier that we use in

00:03:03.321 --> 00:03:04.820
this particular
experiment was fastText

00:03:04.821 --> 00:03:06.290
which is not the self text

00:03:06.291 --> 00:03:08.240
categorization model
from Facebook,

00:03:08.241 --> 00:03:10.685
models as words as bigrams,

00:03:10.686 --> 00:03:12.245
unigrams, very, very simple.

00:03:12.246 --> 00:03:15.065
We were able to get
67 percent accuracy on SNLI,

00:03:15.066 --> 00:03:17.890
and about 55 percent
accuracy on multiNLI.

00:03:17.891 --> 00:03:18.960
This is kind of harrowing.

00:03:18.961 --> 00:03:20.150
This tells us that
those datasets aren't

00:03:20.151 --> 00:03:22.435
really doing what they're
supposed to be doing.

00:03:22.436 --> 00:03:24.685
We don't really need to
learn entailment to perform

00:03:24.686 --> 00:03:27.825
about 2x above
our majority baselines.

00:03:27.826 --> 00:03:30.290
So, the sort of projects

00:03:30.291 --> 00:03:32.830
that we embarked on
was really to answer

00:03:32.831 --> 00:03:34.780
this question of why
we're able to do so

00:03:34.781 --> 00:03:37.625
well on this task with
just the hypothesis.

00:03:37.626 --> 00:03:40.360
We showed that elicited
annotations induce

00:03:40.361 --> 00:03:42.100
lexical clues which we call

00:03:42.101 --> 00:03:45.505
annotation artifacts that
reveal the entailment class.

00:03:45.506 --> 00:03:48.190
We showed that supervised models,

00:03:48.191 --> 00:03:49.330
especially the
top-performing ones,

00:03:49.331 --> 00:03:52.265
exploit these annotation
artifacts in making predictions.

00:03:52.266 --> 00:03:54.819
We present the wider implications

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:57.385
for model evaluation
and task design.

00:03:57.386 --> 00:03:59.910
So, even though this talk
is very centered

00:03:59.911 --> 00:04:02.580
around NLI data, hopefully,

00:04:02.581 --> 00:04:05.100
anyone who's working with
creating datasets using

00:04:05.101 --> 00:04:07.260
crowdsource annotators
or evaluating models

00:04:07.261 --> 00:04:09.945
in general can benefit
from these results.

00:04:09.946 --> 00:04:11.910
So, the first argument
we had in our paper was

00:04:11.911 --> 00:04:13.815
that there is this

00:04:13.816 --> 00:04:16.380
annotation artifacts
in NLI hypotheses

00:04:16.381 --> 00:04:18.765
that heavily correlate
with the entailment class.

00:04:18.766 --> 00:04:22.980
I'm going to walk through
how we showed that,

00:04:22.981 --> 00:04:25.230
but I think it's prudent to think

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:26.250
about how these data sets are

00:04:26.251 --> 00:04:28.335
generated in the first place.

00:04:28.336 --> 00:04:31.890
So, essentially, SNLI
and multiNLI are

00:04:31.891 --> 00:04:34.785
generated through
sort of captions and

00:04:34.786 --> 00:04:36.750
source texts for multiple genres

00:04:36.751 --> 00:04:39.300
that are fed to annotators on

00:04:39.301 --> 00:04:41.220
MTurk who were then
asked to create

00:04:41.221 --> 00:04:42.840
those three hypotheses:
entailment,

00:04:42.841 --> 00:04:44.400
contradiction, and neutral.

00:04:44.401 --> 00:04:46.545
There's a real focus
on scale here,

00:04:46.546 --> 00:04:47.910
both from the annotators'

00:04:47.911 --> 00:04:50.025
perspective as well as the task
designers' perspective.

00:04:50.026 --> 00:04:52.320
The task designer wants
as many examples as

00:04:52.321 --> 00:04:53.550
possible so we can feed those in

00:04:53.551 --> 00:04:55.365
our data hungry neural nets,

00:04:55.366 --> 00:04:58.890
and annotators want to
maximize their word,

00:04:58.891 --> 00:05:01.275
annotate as many
examples as possible.

00:05:01.276 --> 00:05:03.480
SNLI and multiNLI achieved

00:05:03.481 --> 00:05:06.810
about one million
high-agreeing examples,

00:05:06.811 --> 00:05:08.340
and this is really
great for that,

00:05:08.341 --> 00:05:10.290
for this particular area
of NLP because

00:05:10.291 --> 00:05:13.290
previous datasets
either relied on

00:05:13.291 --> 00:05:16.380
expert annotators or relied

00:05:16.381 --> 00:05:17.490
on very specific theories of

00:05:17.491 --> 00:05:20.040
entailment that were
very hard to scale.

00:05:20.041 --> 00:05:22.875
In this paper, under
this framework,

00:05:22.876 --> 00:05:26.000
we showed that
annotators are sort

00:05:26.001 --> 00:05:27.915
of trying to maximize
their efficiency

00:05:27.916 --> 00:05:29.340
in creating these hypotheses,

00:05:29.341 --> 00:05:31.440
and thus introducing
these sort of heuristics and

00:05:31.441 --> 00:05:36.150
tricks to create examples that
our models are trained on.

00:05:36.151 --> 00:05:37.840
So, I'm going to go
walk through some of

00:05:37.841 --> 00:05:39.530
the artifacts that we
found in the paper.

00:05:39.531 --> 00:05:42.250
We quantitatively justify
them in the paper,

00:05:42.251 --> 00:05:43.240
and there are
a lot more artifacts

00:05:43.241 --> 00:05:43.990
that we show in the paper.

00:05:43.991 --> 00:05:46.865
So, if you're interested,
definitely check them out.

00:05:46.866 --> 00:05:50.115
So, entailment artifacts
included generalizations.

00:05:50.116 --> 00:05:53.015
We saw a lot of entailment

00:05:53.016 --> 00:05:54.340
hypotheses that were associated

00:05:54.341 --> 00:05:56.200
with premises like some men and

00:05:56.201 --> 00:05:58.330
boys are playing frisbee
in a grassy area.

00:05:58.331 --> 00:06:00.250
Entailments having
generalizations over

00:06:00.251 --> 00:06:01.510
entities in that text,

00:06:01.511 --> 00:06:03.460
people playing frisbee outdoors.

00:06:03.461 --> 00:06:04.830
We also see shortening where

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:06.895
specific words are
removed from the premise.

00:06:06.896 --> 00:06:09.160
For example here, the word
shirt and the word

00:06:09.161 --> 00:06:13.250
green are removed just to
create a entailment hypothesis.

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:17.370
Neutral artifacts
contained purpose clauses.

00:06:17.371 --> 00:06:20.605
For example, if two dogs are
running through a field,

00:06:20.606 --> 00:06:22.180
the neutral hypothesis that was

00:06:22.181 --> 00:06:25.000
created was some puppies are
running to catch a stick.

00:06:25.001 --> 00:06:26.740
Basically, an ambiguous
situation was created

00:06:26.741 --> 00:06:29.915
by applying a reason
to the premise.

00:06:29.916 --> 00:06:33.310
Modifiers are also really
popular where people just

00:06:33.311 --> 00:06:35.320
stuck sort of adjectives

00:06:35.321 --> 00:06:37.330
or superlatives in
front entities in the text,

00:06:37.331 --> 00:06:41.870
in the premise to sort of
create the neutral hypothesis.

00:06:41.871 --> 00:06:44.690
Contradictions have very,
very consistent trends,

00:06:44.691 --> 00:06:46.435
negation was really popular.

00:06:46.436 --> 00:06:48.400
This is one of
my favorite examples because

00:06:48.401 --> 00:06:51.010
the premise is very intricate
and like complicated,

00:06:51.011 --> 00:06:52.385
has a lot of stuff going on.

00:06:52.386 --> 00:06:54.150
But the contradiction
that they chose was

00:06:54.151 --> 00:06:56.040
nobody has a cat
which is like really,

00:06:56.041 --> 00:06:58.590
really simple and it's
a valid form of entailment,

00:06:58.591 --> 00:06:59.850
but it's something that
models can really,

00:06:59.851 --> 00:07:01.470
really easily pick up on.

00:07:01.471 --> 00:07:04.770
Cats are also really popular
in the contradictions.

00:07:04.771 --> 00:07:06.480
The Flickr30k dataset,
which is what

00:07:06.481 --> 00:07:08.700
the SNLI data was derived from,

00:07:08.701 --> 00:07:10.485
contains a lot of dogs.

00:07:10.486 --> 00:07:12.750
So, people thought cats
were contrary to dogs,

00:07:12.751 --> 00:07:13.875
I sort of disagree with that,

00:07:13.876 --> 00:07:15.720
having a cat and a dog.

00:07:15.721 --> 00:07:18.180
But this is sort of

00:07:18.181 --> 00:07:20.760
the human belief and
the sort of biases that

00:07:20.761 --> 00:07:23.520
get imbued into the dataset

00:07:23.521 --> 00:07:26.640
based on people's
previous experience.

00:07:26.641 --> 00:07:30.230
So, we've shown that
artifacts exists,

00:07:30.231 --> 00:07:32.650
and the next argument
of the paper

00:07:32.651 --> 00:07:35.260
is that supervised models,

00:07:35.261 --> 00:07:36.610
especially the
top-performing ones,

00:07:36.611 --> 00:07:39.350
exploit them to
make a predictions.

00:07:39.351 --> 00:07:41.440
The way that we
showed this, is we

00:07:41.441 --> 00:07:42.600
revisited the original model

00:07:42.601 --> 00:07:43.980
that motivated all this work.

00:07:43.981 --> 00:07:45.575
The hypothesis-only
classifier that

00:07:45.576 --> 00:07:46.450
we're able to do really

00:07:46.451 --> 00:07:49.850
well on SNLI with
and multiNLI with.

00:07:49.851 --> 00:07:52.480
We basically fed in the test data

00:07:52.481 --> 00:07:55.135
for both multiNLI and SNLI,

00:07:55.136 --> 00:07:58.210
and if the hypothesis-only
classifier was

00:07:58.211 --> 00:07:59.500
able to get that example

00:07:59.501 --> 00:08:01.210
right by just using
the hypothesis,

00:08:01.211 --> 00:08:02.830
we consider that example easy.

00:08:02.831 --> 00:08:05.050
If it wasn't able to do it,

00:08:05.051 --> 00:08:06.820
so we consider it hard.

00:08:06.821 --> 00:08:08.425
So, in this way, we partition

00:08:08.426 --> 00:08:11.215
the test data into
hard and easy subsets,

00:08:11.216 --> 00:08:14.230
and we also revisited
the models that I

00:08:14.231 --> 00:08:17.680
originally showed that did
well on SNLI and multiNLI.

00:08:17.681 --> 00:08:20.200
We basically tested
these models on

00:08:20.201 --> 00:08:23.145
the easy and hard subsets
and saw how they did.

00:08:23.146 --> 00:08:24.735
We basically showed that

00:08:24.736 --> 00:08:27.720
easy examples drove
NLI performance.

00:08:27.721 --> 00:08:29.430
If you see a graph here,

00:08:29.431 --> 00:08:31.950
this is the accuracy
of the same models.

00:08:31.951 --> 00:08:34.755
On the full data, we get
about 87 percent accuracy.

00:08:34.756 --> 00:08:36.749
If you look at just
the hard examples,

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:37.950
the ones that hypothesis-only

00:08:37.951 --> 00:08:39.525
classifier was not
able to get correct,

00:08:39.526 --> 00:08:41.040
the accuracy of
those models drops

00:08:41.041 --> 00:08:43.095
pretty significantly,
10 to 20 points.

00:08:43.096 --> 00:08:45.075
If you look the easy examples,

00:08:45.076 --> 00:08:47.850
those models did
really, really well.

00:08:47.851 --> 00:08:48.900
So, this kind of shows us

00:08:48.901 --> 00:08:50.310
the majority of the examples that

00:08:50.311 --> 00:08:51.570
these models are
able to get right or

00:08:51.571 --> 00:08:53.595
the ones that have
annotation artifacts.

00:08:53.596 --> 00:08:55.590
We get very similar results
in multiNLI too,

00:08:55.591 --> 00:08:59.010
check out the paper if
you want to learn more.

00:08:59.011 --> 00:09:00.380
So, given these results,

00:09:00.381 --> 00:09:02.115
I have a few closing thoughts.

00:09:02.116 --> 00:09:05.965
You can think of annotation
artifacts as sampling bias.

00:09:05.966 --> 00:09:08.260
It's possible that, so I mean,

00:09:08.261 --> 00:09:10.270
it's crucial to know that
none of these examples are

00:09:10.271 --> 00:09:12.520
invalid forms of
entailment, right?

00:09:12.521 --> 00:09:15.690
They're all correct, they're
just dominating the dataset,

00:09:15.691 --> 00:09:17.260
these very easy
lexical heuristics

00:09:17.261 --> 00:09:18.560
are dominating the data set.

00:09:18.561 --> 00:09:20.010
So, it's possible that like in

00:09:20.011 --> 00:09:21.465
a good way to solve this problem,

00:09:21.466 --> 00:09:23.215
is to sort of normalize

00:09:23.216 --> 00:09:25.540
the distribution of strategies

00:09:25.541 --> 00:09:27.445
that are used to create
entailment hypothesis.

00:09:27.446 --> 00:09:29.570
Not only using
these lexical heuristics,

00:09:29.571 --> 00:09:31.150
but also using things
like world knowledge

00:09:31.151 --> 00:09:33.070
or political perspective
or other forms

00:09:33.071 --> 00:09:37.455
of entailment that don't
readily exists in that dataset.

00:09:37.456 --> 00:09:40.410
Another really important
thing to realize is

00:09:40.411 --> 00:09:42.850
that the reason this
is a problem is that,

00:09:42.851 --> 00:09:44.890
a lot of these models are
very lexically-driven,

00:09:44.891 --> 00:09:47.495
and they're very susceptible
to these artifacts.

00:09:47.496 --> 00:09:49.970
What we want is a very
general representation of

00:09:49.971 --> 00:09:52.325
entailment that is invariant
to these artifacts.

00:09:52.326 --> 00:09:54.260
But what we see is that
these models are making

00:09:54.261 --> 00:09:56.360
lexical associations
between words and

00:09:56.361 --> 00:09:57.720
surface patterns like Ihashon was

00:09:57.721 --> 00:10:01.035
mentioning, to these labels.

00:10:01.036 --> 00:10:02.590
So we have to really
rethink about

00:10:02.591 --> 00:10:04.940
how we're modeling entailment,

00:10:04.941 --> 00:10:07.600
to try to create
representations that

00:10:07.601 --> 00:10:11.260
are really invariant to
these problems that we see.

00:10:11.570 --> 00:10:14.685
Also this is not just about NLI.

00:10:14.686 --> 00:10:16.690
This paper I think is

00:10:16.691 --> 00:10:18.660
a child of a series of
papers that have come out

00:10:18.661 --> 00:10:21.645
recently thinking
about datasets and

00:10:21.646 --> 00:10:24.930
critiquing how datasets
are either created,

00:10:24.931 --> 00:10:26.050
how models can be easily

00:10:26.051 --> 00:10:29.890
broken by introducing
adversarial examples.

00:10:29.891 --> 00:10:35.180
I think that more work
like this should be done,

00:10:35.181 --> 00:10:37.580
I think it's an important
part of doing NLP that

00:10:37.581 --> 00:10:40.100
we're empirically driven and that

00:10:40.101 --> 00:10:42.500
we're sure that what
we're measuring and

00:10:42.501 --> 00:10:46.040
evaluating models for are
actually what they're learning.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:51.070
Lastly, I think progress in
NLI has been overestimated.

00:10:51.071 --> 00:10:53.410
I think that there are a lot of

00:10:53.411 --> 00:10:56.355
open questions that sort
of result to this work,

00:10:56.356 --> 00:10:59.794
that really focus on how
we do better annotation,

00:10:59.795 --> 00:11:01.830
how can we direct annotators to

00:11:01.831 --> 00:11:04.030
avoid these simple heuristics.

00:11:04.031 --> 00:11:07.315
When generating examples,
can we employ some sort of

00:11:07.316 --> 00:11:10.205
automated real-time sort
of reward system

00:11:10.206 --> 00:11:14.070
that disincentivizes
using simple heuristics.

00:11:14.071 --> 00:11:17.055
Are there alternatives
to elicitation

00:11:17.056 --> 00:11:21.475
that can help us build
entailment datasets?

00:11:21.476 --> 00:11:25.115
I think that there are
a ton more open questions,

00:11:25.116 --> 00:11:26.960
and I'd love to talk
to people about it,

00:11:26.961 --> 00:11:29.580
if you're interested.

00:11:29.581 --> 00:11:32.500
We released the hard benchmarks
on Kaggle and SNLI.

00:11:32.501 --> 00:11:34.755
So If you're interested in
national good inference

00:11:34.756 --> 00:11:36.940
definitely i encourage you
to check these benchmarks

00:11:36.941 --> 00:11:40.390
out and test your models on
both the full benchmarks as

00:11:40.391 --> 00:11:42.185
well as these hard
subsets to see if

00:11:42.186 --> 00:11:44.885
you generalize across
these artifacts.

00:11:44.886 --> 00:11:48.180
Thanks. Here's
the link to my paper.

00:11:54.970 --> 00:11:58.310
&gt;&gt; Okay. We actually have
lots of time for questions.

00:11:58.311 --> 00:12:07.730
Okay.

00:12:07.731 --> 00:12:10.670
&gt;&gt; Thanks. Also are there
interesting patterns to

00:12:10.671 --> 00:12:14.010
the hard examples or
are they more diverse?

00:12:14.011 --> 00:12:17.700
&gt;&gt; Yes. So we didn't
include this in

00:12:17.701 --> 00:12:19.200
the paper because it's

00:12:19.201 --> 00:12:20.445
a very hard problem

00:12:20.446 --> 00:12:22.650
but when you look at
the hard subsets,

00:12:22.651 --> 00:12:24.895
it's not that they don't
contain artifacts,

00:12:24.896 --> 00:12:27.250
it's that they contain
very different types

00:12:27.251 --> 00:12:29.920
of skews from the easy subset.

00:12:29.921 --> 00:12:32.370
So for example, maybe you'd have

00:12:32.371 --> 00:12:35.755
a lot of examples in the hard
subset where like "not".

00:12:35.756 --> 00:12:38.630
The word "not",
negation is associated

00:12:38.631 --> 00:12:41.555
with a different class like
neutrals or entailments.

00:12:41.556 --> 00:12:47.605
So it's a kind of an open
question about sort of how

00:12:47.606 --> 00:12:50.180
we utilize "hard training

00:12:50.181 --> 00:12:51.530
data" and the best way is there

00:12:51.531 --> 00:12:53.410
a way to remove these artifacts.

00:12:53.411 --> 00:13:11.145
Yeah, it's hard to tell.

00:13:11.146 --> 00:13:13.910
&gt;&gt; So I was thinking

00:13:13.911 --> 00:13:16.595
specifically with
the example of negation,

00:13:16.596 --> 00:13:19.175
that seems one that's maybe

00:13:19.176 --> 00:13:22.175
would be natural to
contradiction and maybe

00:13:22.176 --> 00:13:25.330
the reason people are making
the sentences like that is

00:13:25.331 --> 00:13:26.600
because that's such a natural way

00:13:26.601 --> 00:13:28.650
to produce contradiction.

00:13:28.651 --> 00:13:31.940
So in that way it seems could

00:13:31.941 --> 00:13:35.325
be a natural bias that you
wouldn't want to remove.

00:13:35.326 --> 00:13:36.610
&gt;&gt; Right. Yes.

00:13:36.611 --> 00:13:38.560
&gt;&gt; Like some of them
like cat obviously.

00:13:38.561 --> 00:13:41.510
That is like a problem from
the initial data is that it

00:13:41.511 --> 00:13:44.740
seems but some of
them seem less. Yes.

00:13:44.741 --> 00:13:46.420
&gt;&gt; Yeah a hundred
percent. I mean,

00:13:46.421 --> 00:13:48.370
none of these examples are

00:13:48.371 --> 00:13:51.590
invalid forms of entailment.
They're all valid.

00:13:51.591 --> 00:13:54.635
I think the key is when
we're evaluating our models,

00:13:54.636 --> 00:13:56.590
we got to make sure
if we are saying

00:13:56.591 --> 00:13:58.855
that we're making a lot
of progress on NLI,

00:13:58.856 --> 00:14:00.460
what are we actually
making progress on.

00:14:00.461 --> 00:14:03.000
Are we making progress on
these easy less cool heuristics

00:14:03.001 --> 00:14:04.430
or are we actually
making progress

00:14:04.431 --> 00:14:05.655
on my General entailment?

00:14:05.656 --> 00:14:07.100
Right. So it's really

00:14:07.101 --> 00:14:09.180
a question of how we
build our data-sets.

00:14:09.181 --> 00:14:11.170
So we're evaluating our progress

00:14:11.171 --> 00:14:14.180
on these tasks and
the best way possible.

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:28.890
&gt;&gt; So you defined
your easy examples as

00:14:28.891 --> 00:14:30.305
the one that's
your basic model was

00:14:30.306 --> 00:14:32.335
able to classify correctly.

00:14:32.336 --> 00:14:34.820
But do you make a difference
between the ones that

00:14:34.821 --> 00:14:37.120
the basic examples
are that actually

00:14:37.121 --> 00:14:38.280
easy versus the ones that

00:14:38.281 --> 00:14:40.510
the model is picked up by chance?

00:14:40.511 --> 00:14:43.550
&gt;&gt; We don't make
that distinction.

00:14:43.551 --> 00:14:46.010
I think there's
actually some work

00:14:46.011 --> 00:14:49.460
that my co-authors while
those about to work

00:14:49.461 --> 00:14:52.260
on with creating
better partitions of

00:14:52.261 --> 00:14:55.525
easy and hard subsets,

00:14:55.526 --> 00:14:58.850
by using maybe like
an adversarial model that is

00:14:58.851 --> 00:15:02.570
sort of online trying to

00:15:02.571 --> 00:15:05.450
classify both easy
and hard examples

00:15:05.451 --> 00:15:06.760
while another model is trying to

00:15:06.761 --> 00:15:11.980
actually perform entailment
and sort of predictions.

00:15:11.981 --> 00:15:13.410
I think this is really

00:15:13.411 --> 00:15:15.200
just a good first step
into trying to

00:15:15.201 --> 00:15:17.415
build a better easy hard subset.

00:15:17.416 --> 00:15:19.790
But there's definitely
a lot more work to be done

00:15:19.791 --> 00:15:23.400
to create those partitions
more accurately.

00:15:33.220 --> 00:15:36.030
&gt;&gt; So you said
this paper was part of

00:15:36.031 --> 00:15:38.020
kind of a series of papers.

00:15:38.021 --> 00:15:41.530
I'm just kind of curious,

00:15:41.531 --> 00:15:44.200
is there other research
going on into artefacts and

00:15:44.201 --> 00:15:46.205
annotations potentially
for different problems

00:15:46.206 --> 00:15:48.215
but similar data sources?

00:15:48.216 --> 00:15:52.550
&gt;&gt; Yes. I personally haven't
seen much work in this area,

00:15:52.551 --> 00:15:56.000
I think there's
generally been a lot of

00:15:56.001 --> 00:15:59.760
work on how- there was actually

00:15:59.761 --> 00:16:02.220
a lot of parallel work
that had been done

00:16:02.221 --> 00:16:04.700
with other groups on
the analyte dataset showing

00:16:04.701 --> 00:16:07.304
that not only are there biases

00:16:07.305 --> 00:16:10.680
with these sort of
heuristics that people use,

00:16:10.681 --> 00:16:13.890
there's also biases in
the context of like,

00:16:13.891 --> 00:16:16.650
people are introducing
stereotypes into

00:16:16.651 --> 00:16:22.085
the data and I think those
are separate problems.

00:16:22.086 --> 00:16:24.240
I'd say that this is definitely

00:16:24.241 --> 00:16:26.310
nascent work and there

00:16:26.311 --> 00:16:28.045
need to be some more work
done in this area too.

00:16:28.046 --> 00:16:28.870
&gt;&gt; Awesome. Thanks.

00:16:28.871 --> 00:16:30.230
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:33.630
&gt;&gt; Okay. One last question.

00:16:45.950 --> 00:16:49.250
&gt;&gt; More of a statement
than a question.

00:16:49.251 --> 00:16:51.050
One strategy we've had to weigh,

00:16:51.051 --> 00:16:55.165
we rely on Mechanical Turk
for a lot of language data.

00:16:55.166 --> 00:16:57.620
One strategy we've had is to

00:16:57.621 --> 00:17:00.820
actually get users to
filter themselves out,

00:17:00.821 --> 00:17:02.045
so you get them,

00:17:02.046 --> 00:17:05.940
you ask them questions that
can become wrong or say

00:17:05.941 --> 00:17:08.625
their answers have to be

00:17:08.626 --> 00:17:13.434
XYZ length to your negation
for example, for instance.

00:17:13.435 --> 00:17:15.150
There might be

00:17:15.151 --> 00:17:17.385
further research and
strategies into that,

00:17:17.386 --> 00:17:20.175
I suppose. Any thoughts on that?

00:17:20.176 --> 00:17:25.740
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I feel like
nowadays there's

00:17:25.741 --> 00:17:28.145
a lot of friction in trying to

00:17:28.146 --> 00:17:31.490
create the real-time interaction
between annotators,

00:17:31.491 --> 00:17:35.025
and when you're creating
these really huge data sets,

00:17:35.026 --> 00:17:36.820
a lot of times you're
not able to identify

00:17:36.821 --> 00:17:38.685
these problems until
after the fact's,

00:17:38.686 --> 00:17:40.630
and then it's too costly to go

00:17:40.631 --> 00:17:42.505
back and recreate the data set.

00:17:42.506 --> 00:17:48.155
So, I think real-time
way to review things,

00:17:48.156 --> 00:17:50.550
examples in batches,
or something like you

00:17:50.551 --> 00:17:53.245
propose would be
really influential.

00:17:53.246 --> 00:17:54.890
&gt;&gt; It's an expensive problem.

00:17:54.891 --> 00:17:55.770
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:17:55.771 --> 00:17:58.160
&gt;&gt; Antoine, are you ready?

00:17:58.161 --> 00:18:01.210
&gt;&gt; Okay, let's thank
the speaker then.

00:18:07.700 --> 00:18:10.350
&gt;&gt; Suchin sort of teach
a lot of what I'm going

00:18:10.351 --> 00:18:12.275
to talk about in this talk,

00:18:12.276 --> 00:18:14.420
which is pretty much
Neural Networks,

00:18:14.421 --> 00:18:18.980
mouths without brains, also
listeners without brains,

00:18:18.981 --> 00:18:21.270
because it goes both ways and

00:18:21.271 --> 00:18:24.550
I'm going to talk about how
we can give them a brain.

00:18:24.551 --> 00:18:27.560
So, Yejin talked a lot

00:18:27.561 --> 00:18:30.005
about recipes and I'm
going to do so as well,

00:18:30.006 --> 00:18:31.620
I usually love to give
this talk right before

00:18:31.621 --> 00:18:34.405
lunch because it's nice
to see people squirm.

00:18:34.406 --> 00:18:36.300
Let's start by having you think

00:18:36.301 --> 00:18:37.905
about making an apple pie.

00:18:37.906 --> 00:18:40.830
When you bake a pie, there's
a process that you follow in

00:18:40.831 --> 00:18:42.810
order to compose
your raw ingredients

00:18:42.811 --> 00:18:44.250
into a final product.

00:18:44.251 --> 00:18:47.225
Intermediate steps such
as slicing apples,

00:18:47.226 --> 00:18:49.190
cooking them, placing them in

00:18:49.191 --> 00:18:51.540
a pie sheet and then
baking the pie,

00:18:51.541 --> 00:18:52.670
all have to be correctly

00:18:52.671 --> 00:18:54.840
navigated in the right order
and with the right

00:18:54.841 --> 00:18:57.120
intermediate constructs if
you're going to make something

00:18:57.121 --> 00:19:00.610
that's both edible and tasty.

00:19:00.611 --> 00:19:04.550
Understanding that process
requires many common senses

00:19:04.551 --> 00:19:08.095
causal inferences to be made
and remembered later on.

00:19:08.096 --> 00:19:10.120
First one's we slice the apples,

00:19:10.121 --> 00:19:11.650
we have to understand some notion

00:19:11.651 --> 00:19:13.275
that their shape has changed.

00:19:13.276 --> 00:19:15.170
When we saute them for a bit,

00:19:15.171 --> 00:19:17.610
we now know that
their cookedness has

00:19:17.611 --> 00:19:19.540
changed and that there
temperature

00:19:19.541 --> 00:19:21.520
has also likely
changed to be warm.

00:19:21.521 --> 00:19:24.170
Also despite having
caused these new changes,

00:19:24.171 --> 00:19:25.390
we also still have to remember

00:19:25.391 --> 00:19:26.850
that they've been
cut from before,

00:19:26.851 --> 00:19:29.125
that shape is still the same.

00:19:29.126 --> 00:19:31.990
We're not going back to
having a raw apple that's

00:19:31.991 --> 00:19:34.490
had a completely new set
of things done to it.

00:19:34.491 --> 00:19:36.575
Then when we add them
to the pie sheet,

00:19:36.576 --> 00:19:39.315
we have to understand that
there's actually a new entity

00:19:39.316 --> 00:19:42.810
that's been created from our
sliced and cooked apples,

00:19:42.811 --> 00:19:46.130
and the dough that we've
now combined it with.

00:19:46.131 --> 00:19:48.180
Finally, once we throw
it into the oven,

00:19:48.181 --> 00:19:50.670
we have to know that
that combination of apples and

00:19:50.671 --> 00:19:53.675
pie sheet is now
going to be cooked.

00:19:53.676 --> 00:19:55.725
So, if we think about how

00:19:55.726 --> 00:19:59.740
current intelligent agents
might parses this information,

00:19:59.741 --> 00:20:02.595
current ubiquitous
approach using LSTM,

00:20:02.596 --> 00:20:05.140
encode all the words
into some type of

00:20:05.141 --> 00:20:07.170
sentence representation
that's usually

00:20:07.171 --> 00:20:09.420
a single vector or
a group of vectors,

00:20:09.421 --> 00:20:12.650
and you hope that that
information can then be queried

00:20:12.651 --> 00:20:16.545
from that representation
in a later step.

00:20:16.546 --> 00:20:18.220
That's not necessarily
what happens though,

00:20:18.221 --> 00:20:21.425
while LSTMs are powerful
representational tools,

00:20:21.426 --> 00:20:23.080
they don't do much more

00:20:23.081 --> 00:20:25.370
than encodes surface
word patterns,

00:20:25.371 --> 00:20:27.520
and they generally haven't
been shown to capture

00:20:27.521 --> 00:20:29.920
more complex world
dynamics that might be

00:20:29.921 --> 00:20:31.290
necessary for reasoning or

00:20:31.291 --> 00:20:33.620
some other downstream task
that you want.

00:20:33.621 --> 00:20:35.994
Some more complex models;

00:20:35.995 --> 00:20:38.305
memory networks or
entity networks,

00:20:38.306 --> 00:20:39.500
do allow for more

00:20:39.501 --> 00:20:41.620
fine-grained state
representations because

00:20:41.621 --> 00:20:43.640
they generally split
this encoded context

00:20:43.641 --> 00:20:45.015
across memory cells,

00:20:45.016 --> 00:20:46.330
which might allow you to retain

00:20:46.331 --> 00:20:47.935
processed information better

00:20:47.936 --> 00:20:50.040
or encodes certain parts

00:20:50.041 --> 00:20:52.560
of your contexts
in different ways.

00:20:52.561 --> 00:20:54.500
But they still don't capture

00:20:54.501 --> 00:20:55.810
any information beyond what's

00:20:55.811 --> 00:20:57.190
explicitly stated in the text,

00:20:57.191 --> 00:20:59.140
they're just learning
text patterns and

00:20:59.141 --> 00:21:01.350
learning how to represent
those in a particular way.

00:21:01.351 --> 00:21:04.250
So, problem with
these approaches then is that,

00:21:04.251 --> 00:21:06.260
the models we're building
aren't ones that

00:21:06.261 --> 00:21:08.665
currently learn
contexts representations,

00:21:08.666 --> 00:21:10.710
that track the state of
the world with all of

00:21:10.711 --> 00:21:12.080
the information that's needed

00:21:12.081 --> 00:21:14.390
to perform downstream tasks.

00:21:14.391 --> 00:21:17.030
Most neural state
representations encode

00:21:17.031 --> 00:21:19.730
only surface patterns
between tokens and they

00:21:19.731 --> 00:21:22.440
generally devolve into encoding
the most simple pattern

00:21:22.441 --> 00:21:23.820
that they can in order to get

00:21:23.821 --> 00:21:25.755
at the tasks that
they're trying to do.

00:21:25.756 --> 00:21:28.150
So, what might be a strong
enough representation for

00:21:28.151 --> 00:21:30.010
some applications might not

00:21:30.011 --> 00:21:31.840
be good for another one
right after,

00:21:31.841 --> 00:21:34.180
and so the reasoning that
you use in one application

00:21:34.181 --> 00:21:37.050
might not be useful for what
happens next in another one.

00:21:37.051 --> 00:21:38.830
So, what we really want

00:21:38.831 --> 00:21:40.760
ultimately is for
the representations that

00:21:40.761 --> 00:21:42.445
we learn to understand some

00:21:42.446 --> 00:21:44.355
underlying dynamic
about the world,

00:21:44.356 --> 00:21:45.470
and not just a dynamic

00:21:45.471 --> 00:21:47.775
about the task that
we're training it on.

00:21:47.776 --> 00:21:50.290
So, this is why we
decided to approach

00:21:50.291 --> 00:21:52.880
texts understanding from
different paradigm.

00:21:52.881 --> 00:21:55.085
Understanding
the state of the world

00:21:55.086 --> 00:21:57.640
means understanding that that
world is entity centric,

00:21:57.641 --> 00:21:58.900
and it involves identifying

00:21:58.901 --> 00:22:01.410
the state changes that
those entities experience.

00:22:01.411 --> 00:22:03.590
Many of these state changes
however might be

00:22:03.591 --> 00:22:05.825
knowledge based as
opposed to text-based,

00:22:05.826 --> 00:22:08.030
so instead of being
explicitly mentioned in text,

00:22:08.031 --> 00:22:09.370
there might be an expectation of

00:22:09.371 --> 00:22:10.760
prior common sense knowledge

00:22:10.761 --> 00:22:13.115
that your model
actually needs to know.

00:22:13.116 --> 00:22:15.780
So, in this work
more specifically,

00:22:15.781 --> 00:22:17.790
we're going to address
the following challenges.

00:22:17.791 --> 00:22:19.140
We're going to design a simulator

00:22:19.141 --> 00:22:20.380
that's entity aware and that

00:22:20.381 --> 00:22:21.990
focuses on capturing information

00:22:21.991 --> 00:22:23.710
that describes
changes to entities.

00:22:23.711 --> 00:22:26.185
So, after reading any
sentence in a recipe,

00:22:26.186 --> 00:22:27.580
we're going to choose an entity,

00:22:27.581 --> 00:22:29.860
apply a transformation
to it and then we're

00:22:29.861 --> 00:22:32.525
going to update a running
representation of its state.

00:22:32.526 --> 00:22:35.490
Later, we should be able
to predict attributes of

00:22:35.491 --> 00:22:36.630
the state based on

00:22:36.631 --> 00:22:39.165
an embedding that we're
tracking and storing for.

00:22:39.166 --> 00:22:41.260
Second, we're going to integrate

00:22:41.261 --> 00:22:43.860
common sense knowledge about
physical state changes into

00:22:43.861 --> 00:22:45.870
the neural network by
adding knowledge about

00:22:45.871 --> 00:22:48.415
action causality as
a structural bias.

00:22:48.416 --> 00:22:50.200
So, we're going to
do this by allowing

00:22:50.201 --> 00:22:51.380
a predefined set of

00:22:51.381 --> 00:22:54.620
action embeddings to cause
changes to our entity states.

00:22:54.621 --> 00:22:56.680
So, raw text is actually
only going to be used

00:22:56.681 --> 00:22:58.760
to select entities
and select actions,

00:22:58.761 --> 00:23:00.150
and the actual simulation of

00:23:00.151 --> 00:23:02.490
the events that are described
in the text is going

00:23:02.491 --> 00:23:03.570
to be done by applying

00:23:03.571 --> 00:23:05.135
these learned action functions

00:23:05.136 --> 00:23:07.055
to the entity state embeddings.

00:23:07.056 --> 00:23:09.310
So, then the ultimate
goal is really

00:23:09.311 --> 00:23:11.200
to learn a set of
good actions functions that

00:23:11.201 --> 00:23:13.710
can consistently induce
the correct state changes

00:23:13.711 --> 00:23:17.040
and entities based on the
text that's being read.

00:23:17.240 --> 00:23:20.505
We're going to do this
in a two-step process.

00:23:20.506 --> 00:23:23.420
First, in order to develop
a neural architecture that can

00:23:23.421 --> 00:23:25.210
learn these effects of actions

00:23:25.211 --> 00:23:26.620
that they have on entities,

00:23:26.621 --> 00:23:27.780
we're going to crowdsource a set

00:23:27.781 --> 00:23:29.240
of action state mappings,

00:23:29.241 --> 00:23:30.540
and then we're going
to see how we can use

00:23:30.541 --> 00:23:33.300
this crowdsource information
to learn the parameters of

00:23:33.301 --> 00:23:35.700
a neural network that is
structured to capture

00:23:35.701 --> 00:23:36.975
the inductive bias of how

00:23:36.976 --> 00:23:40.030
actions affect
state changes and entities.

00:23:40.031 --> 00:23:42.340
So, to crowdsource, action-state

00:23:42.341 --> 00:23:44.070
mapping's not
particularly interested,

00:23:44.071 --> 00:23:46.485
but we extracted
a set of 380 verbs

00:23:46.486 --> 00:23:48.120
from a cooking domain that

00:23:48.121 --> 00:23:49.820
are mentioned fairly frequently,

00:23:49.821 --> 00:23:51.680
and then we manually
compiled a set of

00:23:51.681 --> 00:23:54.405
six state changes that
actions could entail.

00:23:54.406 --> 00:23:57.470
So, location,
composition, cleanliness,

00:23:57.471 --> 00:24:00.210
cookedness,
temperature and shape,

00:24:00.211 --> 00:24:03.060
and by using the state changes
as a supervision,

00:24:03.061 --> 00:24:04.730
we can learn the transformations

00:24:04.731 --> 00:24:07.525
that actions should
induce in entities.

00:24:07.526 --> 00:24:09.715
So, for each of the 384 actions,

00:24:09.716 --> 00:24:11.740
we actually crowdsource
steady-state changes

00:24:11.741 --> 00:24:14.095
that action tends to
induce in entities.

00:24:14.096 --> 00:24:16.325
So, for an action like cuts,

00:24:16.326 --> 00:24:18.805
we're generally going to expect
to see a change in shape.

00:24:18.806 --> 00:24:20.415
For an action like boil,

00:24:20.416 --> 00:24:21.310
we're going to see a change in

00:24:21.311 --> 00:24:22.805
cookedness and in temperature,

00:24:22.806 --> 00:24:23.940
so you can have multiple state

00:24:23.941 --> 00:24:25.750
changes for the same action.

00:24:25.751 --> 00:24:28.180
Rinse involves change in

00:24:28.181 --> 00:24:29.810
cleanliness and stirring might

00:24:29.811 --> 00:24:32.210
involve a change in composition.

00:24:32.330 --> 00:24:36.670
Now that we have this action
state mapping knowledge,

00:24:36.671 --> 00:24:38.225
we come up with this model

00:24:38.226 --> 00:24:40.020
which we call
the neural process network

00:24:40.021 --> 00:24:42.590
because it models the processes

00:24:42.591 --> 00:24:44.285
that are described in text,

00:24:44.286 --> 00:24:46.140
which is a new
neural network that

00:24:46.141 --> 00:24:48.990
decomposes the state tracking
task into one of tracking

00:24:48.991 --> 00:24:50.790
actions in entities and learning

00:24:50.791 --> 00:24:52.500
the transformations
that those actions

00:24:52.501 --> 00:24:54.775
are going to induce
in the entities.

00:24:54.776 --> 00:24:56.560
So, to do this,
the model actually

00:24:56.561 --> 00:24:58.630
keeps track of the set
of state embeddings

00:24:58.631 --> 00:25:01.155
for the entities that

00:25:01.156 --> 00:25:02.895
it can change
throughout the process,

00:25:02.896 --> 00:25:04.770
and a known set of
action functions that

00:25:04.771 --> 00:25:06.534
can actually change
those entities,

00:25:06.535 --> 00:25:08.430
and one of which
corresponds to each of

00:25:08.431 --> 00:25:11.620
the actions that we
crowdsourced earlier.

00:25:11.900 --> 00:25:14.250
So, let me go over what
the model is going to

00:25:14.251 --> 00:25:15.740
try to accomplish
to a high level.

00:25:15.741 --> 00:25:17.130
In the interest of
time, I'm not going to

00:25:17.131 --> 00:25:18.710
go into too many
details about it,

00:25:18.711 --> 00:25:20.755
because I really want
to get to the results,

00:25:20.756 --> 00:25:22.900
but I'm actually going to

00:25:22.901 --> 00:25:24.020
focus in on a couple of parts

00:25:24.021 --> 00:25:26.035
that I think are
super interesting.

00:25:26.036 --> 00:25:28.550
So, the first thing that
happens is typical,

00:25:28.551 --> 00:25:30.160
is that it's going to
read in a sentence using

00:25:30.161 --> 00:25:32.420
some type of recurrent
neural network such as

00:25:32.421 --> 00:25:34.230
the GRU and it's going

00:25:34.231 --> 00:25:36.685
to build a sentence
representation for it.

00:25:36.686 --> 00:25:37.470
So, in this case,

00:25:37.471 --> 00:25:38.990
I'm going to use
this running example of cooking

00:25:38.991 --> 00:25:42.380
the beef on the pan to
highlight a couple of things.

00:25:42.381 --> 00:25:46.100
Using this encoded
representation from the encoder,

00:25:46.101 --> 00:25:47.320
it's going to select entities

00:25:47.321 --> 00:25:48.720
that are going to be
changed by the events

00:25:48.721 --> 00:25:51.310
in the sentence using
an attention mechanism,

00:25:51.311 --> 00:25:54.525
that computes a binary choice
over each individual entity

00:25:54.526 --> 00:25:56.480
and this allows us to
choose multiple entities

00:25:56.481 --> 00:25:58.165
to be changed in
a particular sentence.

00:25:58.166 --> 00:26:00.195
So, in this sentence for
cook the beef in the pan,

00:26:00.196 --> 00:26:02.215
it would choose to beef entity.

00:26:02.216 --> 00:26:04.515
Then the model is going
to select an action

00:26:04.516 --> 00:26:06.720
that it thinks is
changing the entities it

00:26:06.721 --> 00:26:08.240
selected earlier in the sentence

00:26:08.241 --> 00:26:09.350
by computing an attention

00:26:09.351 --> 00:26:10.520
over the action functions

00:26:10.521 --> 00:26:12.400
that were initialized
at the start.

00:26:12.401 --> 00:26:14.960
So here, it might select
the action like cook,

00:26:14.961 --> 00:26:16.470
it might also select
an action like

00:26:16.471 --> 00:26:18.090
put because they
recognize the pan is

00:26:18.091 --> 00:26:21.270
usually associated with
the location change.

00:26:21.271 --> 00:26:22.880
Then inside this
applicator function,

00:26:22.881 --> 00:26:24.840
it's actually going to compute
a bi-linear projection

00:26:24.841 --> 00:26:27.450
between this action embedding
and this entity embedding,

00:26:27.451 --> 00:26:31.010
and come up with a new state
embedding that it thinks

00:26:31.011 --> 00:26:32.625
is a reflection of how

00:26:32.626 --> 00:26:35.115
this entity it's selected
earlier has been changed.

00:26:35.116 --> 00:26:36.900
So, in essence, this is the step

00:26:36.901 --> 00:26:39.040
that understands that now,

00:26:39.041 --> 00:26:40.440
some cooking action has been

00:26:40.441 --> 00:26:42.450
applied to the beef from earlier.

00:26:42.451 --> 00:26:45.230
From that, it should actually
be able to tell us what

00:26:45.231 --> 00:26:48.165
about this entity has changed
using a set of classifiers,

00:26:48.166 --> 00:26:49.930
one for each of
the state change types

00:26:49.931 --> 00:26:51.145
that we were tracking earlier.

00:26:51.146 --> 00:26:53.735
So, in this case, the beef
is now hot, it's now cooked.

00:26:53.736 --> 00:26:55.350
Now the pan, meanwhile,

00:26:55.351 --> 00:26:57.760
there hasn't really been
a change in cleanliness at all.

00:26:57.761 --> 00:26:59.970
Finally, it updates
this information

00:26:59.971 --> 00:27:01.275
in the entity state embeddings,

00:27:01.276 --> 00:27:02.670
so the entity states reflect

00:27:02.671 --> 00:27:04.750
the state changes that
the entities have experienced.

00:27:04.751 --> 00:27:08.685
Like I said, this involves
a lot neural networks,

00:27:08.686 --> 00:27:10.410
I'm not going to go into
every single neurons

00:27:10.411 --> 00:27:11.800
specifically but

00:27:11.801 --> 00:27:13.730
here's a couple of things I
want to highlight about it.

00:27:13.731 --> 00:27:15.390
First, when the model predicts

00:27:15.391 --> 00:27:17.525
the state changes that
in entities experienced,

00:27:17.526 --> 00:27:20.930
the errors from predicting
those state changes get

00:27:20.931 --> 00:27:22.490
back-propagated
back all the way to

00:27:22.491 --> 00:27:25.085
the action embeddings
in the action selector,

00:27:25.086 --> 00:27:25.990
which allows us to learn

00:27:25.991 --> 00:27:27.510
representations of
actions that are

00:27:27.511 --> 00:27:28.640
more likely to induce

00:27:28.641 --> 00:27:30.785
the correct state changes
in the future.

00:27:30.786 --> 00:27:33.440
Second point I want to highlight
is that when we update

00:27:33.441 --> 00:27:35.575
the entity state
representations in the memory,

00:27:35.576 --> 00:27:38.490
we update each slot proportional

00:27:38.491 --> 00:27:39.720
to what it's original attention

00:27:39.721 --> 00:27:41.640
was when we selected that entity,

00:27:41.641 --> 00:27:43.760
and what this does is that
it allows us to model

00:27:43.761 --> 00:27:45.750
interesting compositional
effects when

00:27:45.751 --> 00:27:47.520
two entities are
mixed, for example.

00:27:47.521 --> 00:27:50.430
So, because both slots might
be selected at 100 percent,

00:27:50.431 --> 00:27:52.030
we're going to overwrite
both of them with

00:27:52.031 --> 00:27:54.320
the same embedding that
we got at the output,

00:27:54.321 --> 00:27:55.420
which allows us to pretty much

00:27:55.421 --> 00:27:56.560
reflect that these two things are

00:27:56.561 --> 00:27:57.760
now the same entity because

00:27:57.761 --> 00:27:59.155
they've been combined
in a mixture.

00:27:59.156 --> 00:28:01.870
The last thing I want

00:28:01.871 --> 00:28:04.635
to point out is this idea
of recurrent attention.

00:28:04.636 --> 00:28:05.870
So, when it's sentence such as

00:28:05.871 --> 00:28:07.085
"Cook the beef in the pan",

00:28:07.086 --> 00:28:09.910
it's pretty clear which entities
that should be the beef.

00:28:09.911 --> 00:28:11.760
But if I all of a sudden
changed that sentence to

00:28:11.761 --> 00:28:14.005
"cook it in a pan",
all of a sudden,

00:28:14.006 --> 00:28:16.440
it could refer to
pretty much any entity,

00:28:16.441 --> 00:28:17.740
it really depends
on what has been

00:28:17.741 --> 00:28:20.470
seen earlier because
it's a co-reference.

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:22.910
So, what we want to do

00:28:22.911 --> 00:28:25.020
instead is that
we're going to allow

00:28:25.021 --> 00:28:28.310
the model to attend to
previous attention steps,

00:28:28.311 --> 00:28:29.590
so that it can select entities

00:28:29.591 --> 00:28:31.120
that have been selected prior,

00:28:31.121 --> 00:28:33.085
an attention over attentions,

00:28:33.086 --> 00:28:34.580
in order to be able to select

00:28:34.581 --> 00:28:36.235
entities that were seen before.

00:28:36.236 --> 00:28:38.350
So, there is

00:28:38.351 --> 00:28:41.220
a lot of details around
Datasets and training,

00:28:41.221 --> 00:28:43.210
but I encourage you to
look at the paper for

00:28:43.211 --> 00:28:46.580
those it's a very interesting
paper I wrote it,

00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:50.245
but what we've done here,

00:28:50.246 --> 00:28:51.990
is we've sort of done at

00:28:51.991 --> 00:28:54.330
a high level is to build
a simulator that captures

00:28:54.331 --> 00:28:56.680
the Dynamics of
actions being taken

00:28:56.681 --> 00:28:59.175
to the cooking domain as
it reads recipe lines.

00:28:59.176 --> 00:29:00.900
So the underlying task
is really to read

00:29:00.901 --> 00:29:01.910
a recipe and be able to

00:29:01.911 --> 00:29:03.600
predict the entities
that are changed,

00:29:03.601 --> 00:29:06.430
and the state changes that
they undergo in each step.

00:29:06.431 --> 00:29:08.390
So, obviously this leads to

00:29:08.391 --> 00:29:10.070
a great task of
state change prediction,

00:29:10.071 --> 00:29:11.690
and we use the accuracy and F1

00:29:11.691 --> 00:29:13.335
of predicting the correct
state changes,

00:29:13.336 --> 00:29:14.710
and we weigh them by the accuracy

00:29:14.711 --> 00:29:16.425
of predicting
the correct entities,

00:29:16.426 --> 00:29:18.180
and were better than competitive

00:29:18.181 --> 00:29:20.175
Neural baselines such as a GRU,

00:29:20.176 --> 00:29:24.200
which kind of we would
expect to be, as well as,

00:29:24.201 --> 00:29:25.920
the recurrent Entity Network
which is one of

00:29:25.921 --> 00:29:27.090
the best models on the bAbi

00:29:27.091 --> 00:29:29.170
datasets from
machine comprehension.

00:29:29.171 --> 00:29:31.705
When we look at how well
we choose entities,

00:29:31.706 --> 00:29:35.310
we see where this improvement
actually comes from,

00:29:35.311 --> 00:29:38.360
so in the leftmost column
we see that we have

00:29:38.361 --> 00:29:39.920
a much better F1 score
of predicting

00:29:39.921 --> 00:29:43.660
the correct entities
at every step,

00:29:43.661 --> 00:29:45.850
and this actually is not

00:29:45.851 --> 00:29:48.575
necessarily because of how
well we predict raw entities,

00:29:48.576 --> 00:29:50.260
so we do do a bit
better when we look at

00:29:50.261 --> 00:29:52.164
recall of predicting
the raw ingredients,

00:29:52.165 --> 00:29:53.610
compared to the two baselines,

00:29:53.611 --> 00:29:55.320
but it really, which is

00:29:55.321 --> 00:29:56.740
raw ingredients would be
something like predicting

00:29:56.741 --> 00:29:58.015
cut the tomatoes,

00:29:58.016 --> 00:29:59.330
and even something like

00:29:59.331 --> 00:30:01.825
co-reference or
lighted arguments,

00:30:01.826 --> 00:30:03.610
but the big difference
is really how we

00:30:03.611 --> 00:30:05.915
do in predicting
composed entities.

00:30:05.916 --> 00:30:07.640
So, if you think
about something like

00:30:07.641 --> 00:30:09.580
combine the water
and flour in a bowl,

00:30:09.581 --> 00:30:11.630
at the next step, if
know we have some

00:30:11.631 --> 00:30:13.725
like stir the mixture
until dough forms.

00:30:13.726 --> 00:30:15.370
It's really tough to know that

00:30:15.371 --> 00:30:18.170
that dough refers to
this water and this flour,

00:30:18.171 --> 00:30:19.670
and we pretty much
double performance

00:30:19.671 --> 00:30:20.850
that these baselines can actually

00:30:20.851 --> 00:30:22.030
manage at predicting

00:30:22.031 --> 00:30:23.705
these types of
compositional entities,

00:30:23.706 --> 00:30:26.480
which is going to help us
do a better job of tracking

00:30:26.481 --> 00:30:29.170
the state of entities overall.

00:30:29.171 --> 00:30:30.880
Just looking at examples here,

00:30:30.881 --> 00:30:32.340
melt butter in medium saucepan

00:30:32.341 --> 00:30:33.725
overheating the first step.

00:30:33.726 --> 00:30:35.000
Most models should be able to

00:30:35.001 --> 00:30:36.530
predict that the
butter's being changed,

00:30:36.531 --> 00:30:38.315
and the second step
removed from heat,

00:30:38.316 --> 00:30:39.670
there's an elided argument,

00:30:39.671 --> 00:30:41.510
but we're able to
predict that anyway.

00:30:41.511 --> 00:30:43.260
The third step's stir in oats,

00:30:43.261 --> 00:30:45.265
sugar, flour, all
these fun things,

00:30:45.266 --> 00:30:46.750
we still understand
that the butter

00:30:46.751 --> 00:30:48.340
is still there and

00:30:48.341 --> 00:30:50.185
needs to be combined
with these things.

00:30:50.186 --> 00:30:52.575
If we look at something
even more complex.

00:30:52.576 --> 00:30:54.090
Step one, brown meat in oil

00:30:54.091 --> 00:30:55.560
until it's brown on all sides.

00:30:55.561 --> 00:30:56.850
Step two, pour off grease.

00:30:56.851 --> 00:30:58.770
We actually get this wrong,
we predict that the oil

00:30:58.771 --> 00:31:01.250
should leave the mixture
at this point,

00:31:01.251 --> 00:31:02.690
you know the gold says none,

00:31:02.691 --> 00:31:04.380
but if you think
about grease and oil,

00:31:04.381 --> 00:31:07.750
probably fairly similar
embeddings, overall.

00:31:07.751 --> 00:31:11.579
And so, it's conceivable

00:31:11.580 --> 00:31:13.580
that it could have
left at the same time.

00:31:13.581 --> 00:31:16.970
Then in the next step nothing
particularly interesting,

00:31:16.971 --> 00:31:20.440
we predict all these
directly named entities,

00:31:20.441 --> 00:31:23.405
but then the interesting part
is in the step four,

00:31:23.406 --> 00:31:25.820
where the line is bring to
boil and turn very low.

00:31:25.821 --> 00:31:27.845
Cover, simmer
about one and half hours

00:31:27.846 --> 00:31:29.525
or until meat is tender.

00:31:29.526 --> 00:31:31.930
Meat should be fairly easy to

00:31:31.931 --> 00:31:33.845
predict as an ingredient
that's being used here,

00:31:33.846 --> 00:31:35.350
it's explicitly
mentioned in the text,

00:31:35.351 --> 00:31:37.450
what's interesting is that
we actually predict all of

00:31:37.451 --> 00:31:39.090
these other ingredients
that were combined

00:31:39.091 --> 00:31:40.950
with the meat in
the previous step.

00:31:40.951 --> 00:31:43.080
So it's actually
managed to figure out

00:31:43.081 --> 00:31:43.730
that this is actually

00:31:43.731 --> 00:31:45.310
a composition of
all these entities,

00:31:45.311 --> 00:31:48.020
and it predicts them all
together at the same time.

00:31:48.021 --> 00:31:50.800
And to show this effect
a bit more quantitatively,

00:31:50.801 --> 00:31:53.140
if you remember how we
select an update entities,

00:31:53.141 --> 00:31:54.449
which is a set of entities,

00:31:54.450 --> 00:31:55.740
we average them and then we apply

00:31:55.741 --> 00:31:56.985
the action function to them,

00:31:56.986 --> 00:31:58.330
and then we write
the new embedding

00:31:58.331 --> 00:32:00.280
back to the correct memory slot,

00:32:00.281 --> 00:32:02.630
weighted by the original
attention on that slot.

00:32:02.631 --> 00:32:04.280
So, if two entities are selected,

00:32:04.281 --> 00:32:06.320
their embedding
representations should

00:32:06.321 --> 00:32:07.490
usually move a lot closer

00:32:07.491 --> 00:32:08.905
to each other in embedding space,

00:32:08.906 --> 00:32:10.200
because they're both
being partially

00:32:10.201 --> 00:32:11.695
overwritten by the same vector.

00:32:11.696 --> 00:32:13.140
As it turns out if we look at

00:32:13.141 --> 00:32:15.305
the percentage change
in cosine similarity,

00:32:15.306 --> 00:32:17.465
of entity embeddings
that are being combined,

00:32:17.466 --> 00:32:19.370
we can see that they're
generally moving

00:32:19.371 --> 00:32:21.725
a lot closer to each
other in embedding space.

00:32:21.726 --> 00:32:23.450
Now for the skeptics among you,

00:32:23.451 --> 00:32:24.910
you can tell me that that's

00:32:24.911 --> 00:32:26.430
obvious if you select
all of them at

00:32:26.431 --> 00:32:28.140
the same time at the first step
they'll all move

00:32:28.141 --> 00:32:29.995
a lot closer to each
other in embedding space,

00:32:29.996 --> 00:32:31.300
so we should actually
look at which

00:32:31.301 --> 00:32:32.555
ones aren't supposed to do that.

00:32:32.556 --> 00:32:33.910
And it turns out, if we

00:32:33.911 --> 00:32:35.690
superimpose that graph
on this one,

00:32:35.691 --> 00:32:37.590
we can see that if you
just actually select

00:32:37.591 --> 00:32:38.590
entities that aren't supposed to

00:32:38.591 --> 00:32:39.870
be combined in that step,

00:32:39.871 --> 00:32:42.405
they don't move close together
at all in embedding space.

00:32:42.406 --> 00:32:43.730
There's obviously a bit of noise

00:32:43.731 --> 00:32:45.230
that leaks here and there,

00:32:45.231 --> 00:32:47.040
but with a good cut off

00:32:47.041 --> 00:32:49.330
you could probably get
rid of most of that.

00:32:49.331 --> 00:32:51.930
So, we're simulating
entity effects fairly

00:32:51.931 --> 00:32:53.895
nicely, what about actions?

00:32:53.896 --> 00:32:55.620
While this entire model runs on

00:32:55.621 --> 00:32:57.725
the premise that
action functions are applied,

00:32:57.726 --> 00:32:59.690
entity embeddings that
induce state changes,

00:32:59.691 --> 00:33:01.005
if it works correctly,

00:33:01.006 --> 00:33:03.525
we would expect
semantically similar actions

00:33:03.526 --> 00:33:06.090
to end up having similar
action function beddings.

00:33:06.091 --> 00:33:08.000
And the hypothesis is true if we

00:33:08.001 --> 00:33:10.295
compare action functions
and embedding space,

00:33:10.296 --> 00:33:12.260
they end up in
a fairly nice part,

00:33:12.261 --> 00:33:15.910
so the cut action is near
the slice split snap splash,

00:33:15.911 --> 00:33:18.940
mashing ends up being your
spreading pure and squeezing.

00:33:18.941 --> 00:33:21.090
So the internals of
our models seem to be

00:33:21.091 --> 00:33:22.980
looking at the same patterns

00:33:22.981 --> 00:33:25.050
that we're trying to replicate.

00:33:25.051 --> 00:33:27.900
And finally, our
state representations

00:33:27.901 --> 00:33:29.925
have to be useful
for downstream task.

00:33:29.926 --> 00:33:31.740
We have to show there are
similar and can encode

00:33:31.741 --> 00:33:33.380
an advantageous representation of

00:33:33.381 --> 00:33:34.970
the process being described,

00:33:34.971 --> 00:33:37.425
using the world information
that we've provided to it.

00:33:37.426 --> 00:33:39.175
So we applied
our state's Simulation

00:33:39.176 --> 00:33:41.040
Model two recipe step generation.

00:33:41.041 --> 00:33:42.380
So at each step we use

00:33:42.381 --> 00:33:44.035
the state embeddings
of the entities,

00:33:44.036 --> 00:33:45.685
to cede an LSTM generator,

00:33:45.686 --> 00:33:46.690
and we changed it to generate

00:33:46.691 --> 00:33:48.055
the next step in the recipe.

00:33:48.056 --> 00:33:50.055
And across the board, our model

00:33:50.056 --> 00:33:51.755
represented here in Orange,

00:33:51.756 --> 00:33:54.070
does better than
competitive baseline such as

00:33:54.071 --> 00:33:56.100
seek-to-seek or an
attentive seek-to-seek,

00:33:56.101 --> 00:33:58.830
and it's consistent across
metrics such as blue, rouge,

00:33:58.831 --> 00:34:01.755
as well as these
metrics VF1 and SCF1,

00:34:01.756 --> 00:34:03.410
where we pretty much
look at whether we

00:34:03.411 --> 00:34:05.610
generate the correct action
in the next sentence,

00:34:05.611 --> 00:34:08.000
or the correct state change
in the next sentence.

00:34:08.001 --> 00:34:09.480
And interestingly, one of

00:34:09.481 --> 00:34:10.930
the baseline that
we ran was using

00:34:10.931 --> 00:34:12.110
the entity state embeddings from

00:34:12.111 --> 00:34:13.935
the recurrent entity
network tracker.

00:34:13.936 --> 00:34:16.450
So we used the tracker of the
recurrent [inaudible] network that

00:34:16.451 --> 00:34:18.920
baseline which is also sort
of learning this entity.

00:34:18.921 --> 00:34:20.795
This fine-grained
entity representation,

00:34:20.796 --> 00:34:22.460
and we see how well
the state embeddings

00:34:22.461 --> 00:34:24.285
that it encodes due on this task.

00:34:24.286 --> 00:34:26.850
And it does worse than the NMP
and across all metrics.

00:34:26.851 --> 00:34:29.200
Indicating that
the action-oriented simulation

00:34:29.201 --> 00:34:30.990
that we're using
actually plays a role in

00:34:30.991 --> 00:34:33.000
encoding better stay
representations for

00:34:33.001 --> 00:34:35.350
downstream or at generation.

00:34:35.351 --> 00:34:37.330
And a quick example, if we see

00:34:37.331 --> 00:34:38.570
this recipe start generation

00:34:38.571 --> 00:34:40.240
with an ingredient
such as butter,

00:34:40.241 --> 00:34:41.730
you know, the first step
is to preheat

00:34:41.731 --> 00:34:43.650
the oven to 425 degrees.

00:34:43.651 --> 00:34:45.540
These neural networks
immediately jump to

00:34:45.541 --> 00:34:47.360
pretty common answers for

00:34:47.361 --> 00:34:49.220
something that requires
preheating in oven like lately,

00:34:49.221 --> 00:34:50.520
greasing baking pan with oil,

00:34:50.521 --> 00:34:53.315
or combining all ingredients
will mix, and mix well.

00:34:53.316 --> 00:34:54.860
Our model knows that if you have

00:34:54.861 --> 00:34:56.565
some butter, and it's raw,

00:34:56.566 --> 00:34:58.410
you're probably going to melt it,

00:34:58.411 --> 00:35:01.560
which is exactly what
the references is as well.

00:35:01.561 --> 00:35:04.230
So the takeaways text
only gets you so far,

00:35:04.231 --> 00:35:05.820
you really need
a model of the world,

00:35:05.821 --> 00:35:07.380
to do good understanding.

00:35:07.381 --> 00:35:09.810
You can encode these priors

00:35:09.811 --> 00:35:11.395
into a neural network
architecture,

00:35:11.396 --> 00:35:13.410
and that model the world
can be learned from data.

00:35:13.411 --> 00:35:15.780
In future work, we're
looking at stories,

00:35:15.781 --> 00:35:16.560
we're looking at dialogues,

00:35:16.561 --> 00:35:18.250
we're looking at
scientific processes,

00:35:18.251 --> 00:35:19.690
and we're also
looking at what type

00:35:19.691 --> 00:35:20.820
of models of the world can

00:35:20.821 --> 00:35:23.650
we encode to do better on
these downstream tasks.

00:35:23.651 --> 00:35:25.560
And luckily for me,
I work in a group

00:35:25.561 --> 00:35:28.045
that makes a lot of
these models of the world,

00:35:28.046 --> 00:35:29.985
between verb physics,
connotation frames,

00:35:29.986 --> 00:35:31.055
and all these other things.

00:35:31.056 --> 00:35:32.800
There's a lot of data that
we have to actually create

00:35:32.801 --> 00:35:34.900
these world models to do
better in stories, dialogues,

00:35:34.901 --> 00:35:37.060
and scientific processes, so
we're looking at encoding

00:35:37.061 --> 00:35:41.080
those into this
pipeline. Thank you.

